79,"y":0.32786,"w":0.03481,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"L(x, y)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.35155,"w":0.59304,"h":0.0951,"fs":0.01353,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"If we can find a common upper bound M for 0 ƒxx0 , 0 ƒyy0 , and 0 ƒxy0 on a rectangle R centered at (x0, y0) (Figure 14.39), then we can bound the error E throughout R by using a simple formula (derived in Section 14.9). The error is defined by E(x, y) = ƒ(x, y) - L(x, y)."},{"x":0.3634,"y":0.35331,"w":0.5922,"h":0.03129,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"When approximating a differentiable function ƒ(x, y) by its linearization L(x, y) at (x0, y0), an important question is how accurate the approximation might be."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.39603,"w":0.22122,"h":0.01388,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Figure 14.38 The tangent plane"},{"x":0.08857,"y":0.41229,"w":0.22119,"h":0.02819,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"L(x, y) represents the linearization of ƒ(x, y) in Example 5."},{"x":0.39284,"y":0.48635,"w":0.53252,"h":0.09524,"fs":0.01353,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"The Error in the Standard Linear Approximation If ƒ has continuous first and second partial derivatives throughout an open set containing a rectangle R centered at (x0, y0) and if M is any upper bound for the values of 0 ƒxx0 , 0 ƒyy0 , and 0 ƒxy0 on R, then the error E(x, y) incurred in replacing ƒ(x, y) on R by its linearization"},{"x":0.16961,"y":0.56002,"w":0.03924,"h":0.01342,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(x0, y0)"},{"x":0.45861,"y":0.57444,"w":0.39698,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"L(x, y) = ƒ(x0, y0) + ƒx(x0, y0)(x - x0) + ƒy(x0, y0)( y - y0)"},{"x":0.52803,"y":0.59632,"w":0.2605,"h":0.0776,"fs":0.01353,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"0 E(x, y)0 … 1 2 M(0 x - x00 + 0 y - y00 )2."},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.59808,"w":0.14599,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"satisfies the inequality"},{"x":0.09639,"y":0.61112,"w":0.18092,"h":0.01986,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"x 0"},{"x":0.08776,"y":0.63085,"w":0.23775,"h":0.06961,"fs":0.02288,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"R: 0 x - x00 … h, 0 y - y00 … k in the xy-plane."},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.64381,"w":0.24982,"h":0.01388,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Figure 14.39 The rectangular region"},{"x":0.39203,"y":0.65181,"w":0.53708,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"To make 0 E(x, y)0 small for a given M, we just make 0 x - x00 and 0 y - y00 small."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.73154,"w":0.09379,"h":0.01658,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Differentials"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.75588,"w":0.59303,"h":0.03257,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Recall from Section 3.11 that for a function of a single variable, y = ƒ(x), we defined the change in ƒ as x changes from a to a + ∆x by"},{"x":0.57389,"y":0.79381,"w":0.16716,"h":0.01766,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∆ƒ = ƒ(a + ∆x) - ƒ(a)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.81789,"w":0.17177,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and the differential of ƒ as"},{"x":0.60845,"y":0.83985,"w":0.09721,"h":0.01766,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dƒ = ƒ′(a)∆x."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.86393,"w":0.4139,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"We now consider the differential of a function of two variables."},{"x":0.3634,"y":0.87928,"w":0.59222,"h":0.0345,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Suppose a differentiable function ƒ(x, y) and its partial derivatives exist at a point (x0, y0). If we move to a nearby point (x0 + ∆x, y0 + ∆y), the change in ƒ is"},{"x":0.52433,"y":0.91659,"w":0.26626,"h":0.02022,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∆ƒ = ƒ(x0 + ∆x, y0 + ∆y) - ƒ(x0, y0)."}]}